WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.054 --> 00:00:10.920
FAYE MOSS: Hello, and welcome to another episode of Superstars in History , the only talk show that lets you meet some of the most famous people

2
00:00:10.921 --> 00:00:17.985
in history! I'm your host, Faye Moss, and I've got a globetrotting guest for you

3
00:00:17.986 --> 00:00:45.609
today. He was a merchant and explorer who wrote a hit book about his marvelous travels. Please welcome Marco Polo!

4
00:00:45.610 --> 00:00:51.261
MARCO POLO: It's a pleasure to be here. This may be my longest

5
00:00:51.262 --> 00:00:54.940
journey ever. FAYE MOSS: Well, you're no stranger

6
00:00:54.941 --> 00:01:00.399
to long trips. In fact, traveling for extended periods runs

7
00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:08.459
in your family! When you were born in Venice, Italy, around 1254, your father and uncle were away on a

8
00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:13.741
long trip. MARCO POLO: Yes, my father and uncle had left on their journey before

9
00:01:13.742 --> 00:01:22.847
I was born. They were wealthy merchants, you see, and in those days merchants traveled far and wide to find beautiful goods to sell

10
00:01:22.848 --> 00:01:29.929
in Europe. While my father was away, my mother died, so I was raised

11
00:01:29.930 --> 00:01:37.720
by relatives. Years passed, and I was 15 when I met my father for the first time, upon his return with my

12
00:01:37.721 --> 00:01:46.534
uncle. FAYE MOSS: Fifteen? That was a very, very long trip! Did they bring you

13
00:01:46.535 --> 00:01:55.173
any souvenirs? MARCO POLO: They did have a souvenir . . . from the Mongol emperor, Kublai Khan! But it wasn't for me—it was

14
00:01:55.174 --> 00:02:03.517
for the pope. My father and uncle's journey had taken them all the way to Yuan Dynasty China, where they visited

15
00:02:03.518 --> 00:02:10.397
Kublai Khan. The khan was thrilled to meet travelers from Italy since he was curious about the pope and the Catholic

16
00:02:10.398 --> 00:02:19.377
Church. The khan decided to make my father and uncle his ambassadors, and he sent them to Rome with a personal letter for the pope.

17
00:02:19.378 --> 00:02:24.661
FAYE MOSS: The pope must've been pleased to hear from such an

18
00:02:24.662 --> 00:02:32.662
important ruler. MARCO POLO: Actually there was no pope at that time! By the time my father and uncle returned, the

19
00:02:32.663 --> 00:02:39.019
pope had died. The khan was expecting a reply, but it took nearly three years before a new pope

20
00:02:39.020 --> 00:02:47.802
was elected. Finally, with a letter from the new pope to the khan, I set off for China with my father and uncle on the Silk

21
00:02:47.803 --> 00:02:52.787
Road. That was the overland trading route between Europe and Asia.

22
00:02:52.788 --> 00:02:57.582
FAYE MOSS: I bet it wasn’t an easy trip. What was

23
00:02:57.583 --> 00:03:04.370
it like? MARCO POLO: By the thirteenth century, the Silk Road had become very dangerous due to

24
00:03:04.371 --> 00:03:08.119
wars and bandits. We encountered blizzards

25
00:03:08.120 --> 00:03:16.265
and flooded rivers. In the Gobi Desert there was nothing to be found to eat, and it took a month to cross it at its

26
00:03:16.266 --> 00:03:20.672
narrowest point. At its widest, it was said to take a year to

27
00:03:20.673 --> 00:03:29.805
cross. Finally, about three years later, we arrived at Kublai Khan's summer capital, where I met the great khan!

28
00:03:29.806 --> 00:03:37.413
He was delighted that my father and uncle had returned safely, and he welcomed me with enthusiasm.

29
00:03:37.414 --> 00:03:42.082
FAYE MOSS: Tell us more about Kublai Khan! What was he

30
00:03:42.083 --> 00:03:53.558
like? MARCO POLO: Not only did he rule a vast empire, which encompassed a large part of Asia, but he was also very interested in the world beyond

31
00:03:53.559 --> 00:03:58.333
his empire. He welcomed foreigners and the new ideas and knowledge

32
00:03:58.334 --> 00:04:05.131
they brought. He was also tolerant of different religions such as Islam, Buddhism, and

33
00:04:05.132 --> 00:04:16.405
Christianity. It may surprise you, but the Mongol Empire was quite cosmopolitan, with people of different cultures, languages, and religions living within it.

34
00:04:16.406 --> 00:04:22.968
And, if I may say so myself, I impressed the khan with my intelligence. He

35
00:04:22.969 --> 00:04:31.583
made me his envoy, and I traveled around the empire and beyond, seeing the marvels of the world. FAYE MOSS:

36
00:04:31.584 --> 00:04:40.917
Few Europeans had ever traveled to Asia before, so I imagine you saw things that were very different from Europe.

37
00:04:40.918 --> 00:04:48.764
MARCO POLO: Indeed, Faye! In China the cities were well organized with wide, clean

38
00:04:48.765 --> 00:04:56.996
streets. Houses were heated with coal; people bathed every day; and wealthier citizens even had bathrooms in their homes.

39
00:04:56.997 --> 00:05:05.662
Oh, and they had paper money and beautiful porcelain dishes. It was very unlike Europe! FAYE

40
00:05:05.663 --> 00:05:16.102
MOSS: You also traveled to Southeast Asia, where you saw many animals—like rhinoceroses, which you thought were

41
00:05:16.103 --> 00:05:20.384
unicorns! MARCO POLO: Oh yes, those unicorns weren't at all what

42
00:05:20.385 --> 00:05:27.257
I expected. They had thick hair like buffalos, feet like elephants, and heads

43
00:05:27.258 --> 00:05:36.985
like boars! FAYE MOSS: I want to know more about what you saw, but I don't think we have enough time. Your travels

44
00:05:36.986 --> 00:05:47.831
lasted 24 years! MARCO POLO: I almost didn't make it home! Kublai Khan didn't want my father, uncle, and me to leave, and he refused to release me

45
00:05:47.832 --> 00:05:55.222
from his service. Eventually he agreed, but only because he needed us to escort a Mongol

46
00:05:55.223 --> 00:06:06.308
princess to Persia. We left around 1291, traveling by sea. It was a long and dangerous journey, but we arrived safely in Venice four

47
00:06:06.309 --> 00:06:12.719
years later. By then we'd been gone so long, that we struggled to speak our native

48
00:06:12.720 --> 00:06:17.333
language! FAYE MOSS: You came home to war . .

49
00:06:17.334 --> 00:06:24.203
. and prison. MARCO POLO: Yes, Venice was at war with Genoa, so I served my city by buying

50
00:06:24.204 --> 00:06:30.977
and commanding a ship. Sadly I was captured and thrown in prison. Prison wasn't too bad, but

51
00:06:30.978 --> 00:06:35.307
it was boring. I livened things up by telling stories

52
00:06:35.308 --> 00:06:43.014
of my adventures. A fellow prisoner, Rustichello da Pisa, was a famous story writer who wrote down my tales for

53
00:06:43.015 --> 00:06:48.251
a book—but he may have embellished some details to create excitement for readers.

54
00:06:48.252 --> 00:06:53.656
We published the book after we were released from prison around 1299.

55
00:06:53.657 --> 00:06:58.486
It became popular and was even translated into several languages. FAYE

56
00:06:58.487 --> 00:07:06.578
MOSS: That's impressive, Marco—especially since back then every copy of a book had to be written by hand!

57
00:07:06.579 --> 00:07:13.549
But even though your book was widely read, some people were skeptical about the truth of your stories.

58
00:07:13.550 --> 00:07:18.689
MARCO POLO: Yes. Some readers claimed I made things up, but they were

59
00:07:18.690 --> 00:07:25.834
wrong! I really had those adventures! I do admit, however, that I am not a modest man.

60
00:07:25.835 --> 00:07:31.921
I probably overstated a few small details about my influence at the khan's court.

61
00:07:31.922 --> 00:07:38.513
But the records of my travels are quite accurate, and I told only half of what I saw. FAYE

62
00:07:38.514 --> 00:07:45.268
MOSS: Well, your book is certainly well loved—it's stayed in print for over seven hundred years.

63
00:07:45.269 --> 00:07:52.207
Thank you for setting the record straight and for taking another journey . . . this time through history! MARCO

64
00:07:52.208 --> 00:07:59.521
POLO: I enjoyed my visit to your colorful land very

65
00:07:59.522 --> 00:08:06.406
much! FAYE MOSS: Marco Polo spent the rest of his life raising a family and being a successful merchant

66
00:08:06.407 --> 00:08:11.295
in Venice. He died in 1324 at the age

67
00:08:11.296 --> 00:08:20.932
of 69. Polo's book introduced many Europeans to the cultures and customs of Asia, and inspired and educated other

68
00:08:20.933 --> 00:08:33.548
explorers. Thank you for joining us today. I'll see you next time when we talk with another superstar in history.

